// The Enochian Alphabet.
//
// From the wing's research file enochian.verified.json, with its fact-check
// corrections applied and every point it left unresolved either left out or
// stated as uncertain: whose hand drew f. 104, the folio of the second version
// in Sloane MS 3189, Mortlake and Ashmole's date, the extent of the backwards
// dictation, whether Sloane MS 3191 writes the Calls in these letters, the
// Equinox issues and the current Unicode status.
//
// Each glyph is a filled outline ({ fill }) drawn as type on the letter's ink in
// the drawing of 6 May 1583 in Dee's record (Sloane MS 3188, f. 104), as the
// uploaded original of its Wikimedia Commons reproduction (4303 x 1603 px) shows
// it: every letter's strokes laid by hand along the course of its own ink, all
// twenty-one at one scale and on one baseline, given set weights and cut, wedge or
// pointed ends, and the outline held within a few units of the ink's edge. The
// strokes and the checked outlines are kept in the repository:
// docs/research/magical-scriptorium/traced/enochian/f104.glyphs.json (with each
// letter's construction and its measured distance from the ink) and the sources in
// docs/research/magical-scriptorium/traced/enochian/f104.sources.json; the format
// is the glyph kit's (docs/research/magical-scriptorium/glyphkit/README.md,
// "Filled glyphs"). Three letters depart from f. 104 on another witness: the
// crossings of Un and Or, solid in the image, are drawn open on the course of
// Sloane MS 3189 and Casaubon's engraved Holy Table of 1659, and the dot of Pal is
// set clear of its stem, as in Sloane MS 3189; no outline was taken from either.
// No name or value stands beside the forms on f. 104: each form is taken as a
// letter by its place in the order of Dee's table of 26 March 1583 (f. 64b), as
// Whitby's transcription (1982) gives it, which his commentary ties to this
// drawing; f. 64b itself was not seen, and the page says so. The heading of
// Casaubon's Specimen (1659) is cited where it agrees or differs. The register
// keeps the order of the Latin values; each letter's note gives its place on
// f. 104. No plate is shown: the only image of the leaf to hand is a Commons
// reproduction that names the manuscript as its source but not who made the image
// or where it was taken from. Dee is quoted only in a few words of his note above
// the forms, in the spelling of Whitby's transcription.
